{"page_content": "Local environmental distribution\n\nA generic standard environment is defined to allow for a chemical safety assessment at the European level. As it is impossible to characterise an 'average European environment\u2019, default parameter values are chosen which reflect typical or reasonable worst-case settings. Dedicated modelling approaches are used to calculate the concentrations in air, surface water and soil. The sediment and groundwater concentrations are estimated from the surface water and soil concentration respectively.\n\nIn defining the standard environments, a number of assumptions have been made with respect to the spatial and time scales, which are described in Appendix A.16-2.1.\n\nRegional distribution\n\nFor calculating the regional PEC, the multi-media fate-model SimpleBox may be used. The basic characteristics of this model are shown in Figure R.16-6. A description of the assumptions made is given in Appendix R.16-3.\n\nIn the multi-media model used, the environmental media are represented by the following homogeneous and well-mixed compartment 'boxes':\n\nAtmosphere;\n\nSurface water (freshwater and marine environment);\n\nSediment (freshwater and marine environment);\n\nSoil.\n\nR.16-4.1.2 Existing tools for exposure estimation\n\nEUSES (embedded in Chesar Platform) uses models for exposure estimation**. Besides the release estimation, only a few data on substance properties are needed to calculate PECs using EUSES.\n\nThe output data from the tool (to be used in the CSA) are:\n\ne Local and regional PEC values specified in mg/L (water) or mg/kg (soil and sediment).\n\n\u00ab Concentration in food (for the assessment of secondary poisoning) (mg/kg food).\n\ne \u2014 Regional and local total human doses taken up via the environment of the substance.\n\nAt the time of updating this guidance, the most updated version of EUSES is incorporated in Chesar Platform with scientific improvements compared to its standalone version? and therefore the explanation of the fate and transport mechanism in this guidance reflects what is implemented in that version.\n\nMore information on EUSES and other exposure estimation tools that are currently available is provided in Appendix R.16-4.", "metadata": {"doc_id": "f9055e9d-d3c5-4a86-8b5d-1dd56cf265b8", "content_type": "CompositeElement", "filename": "IR_CSR_R16_V4_FINAL.pdf"}}